<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # One, Two, Three Bananas! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Go Shopping!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> One, Two, Three Bananas!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> 
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Receptive: Identify the main idea from short spoken passages.<br>Interactive: Participate in call-and-response activities.</p>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> 
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Listening: Can identify the main idea of short passages about shopping (e.g., "I want five pineapples.").<br>Can participate in call-and-response activities (e.g., call out "Let's go shopping. Walk, walk, walk." Everyone repeats and mimics the action.).<br>Can follow simple instructions at the store (e.g., "Buy three bananas and two oranges.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing new concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a simple TPR "Let's Go Shopping!" chant and action. Teacher says, "Let's go shopping! Walk, walk, walk!" and mimics walking. Students repeat the phrase and mimic the action. The teacher then shows realia (a banana, an apple) or large flashcards of fruits (pineapple, orange, banana, apple) one by one, asking "What's this?" to elicit known vocabulary or introduce new ones.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of new vocabulary: "banana" /bænˈænə/, "pineapple" /ˈpaɪnˌæpəl/, "apple" /ˈæpəl/, "orange" /ˈɒrɪndʒ/. The teacher emphasizes the /b/ sound in "banana" and the /p/ sound in "pineapple" by exaggerating them. The teacher also models phrases for expressing quantity and requesting items using polite expressions, e.g., "I want three bananas, please." while holding up three fingers and pointing to a banana picture. Teacher demonstrates counting "one, two, three" with fingers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up an apple flashcard) "Is this a banana or an apple?" (Expected answer: "An apple.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up two fingers) "How many?" (Expected answer: "Two.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I say 'I want' or 'You want' when I am hungry?" (Expected answer: "I want.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, pre-recorded audio dialogue or teacher-read dialogue between a customer and a cashier, accompanied by large, clear picture flashcards of the fruits and numbers. The dialogue includes target grammar (Number + noun, Present simple) and vocabulary (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, buy, ask, cashier).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b><br>
                    Customer: "Hello! I want three apples, please."<br>
                    Cashier: "Three apples? Here you are! And what else?"<br>
                    Customer: "Can I have one pineapple, please?"<br>
                    Cashier: "One pineapple. That will be ten money. Thank you!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understand short, simple spoken messages and follow basic instructions related to daily needs and shopping for items at an A1.1 level.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue two times.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (1st Listening):</b> "What are they talking about? Food or toys?" (Teacher elicits "Food" or "Shopping for food.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (2nd Listening):</b> Teacher holds up flashcards or draws simple pictures on the board.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The customer wants five apples. True or False?" (Students point to thumbs up/down).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The customer wants one pineapple. True or False?"</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Guided practice of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for specific numbers and fruit names. They will use grammar: Number + noun (e.g., "three bananas"), Present simple ("I want five pineapples."), and numbers (one to ten). Vocabulary: pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money. Pronunciation: focusing on /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple", and phonemic blending for "six" and "money". Pragmatic/Sociolinguistic: Expressing quantity using polite expressions ("I want three apples, please.") and requesting items ("Can I have two bananas, please?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Circle/Draw (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher gives each student a worksheet with pictures of different fruits (e.g., 5 apples, 3 bananas, 2 oranges, 1 pineapple) and numbers. The teacher calls out instructions like, "Circle three bananas," or "Draw two oranges." Students listen carefully and circle the correct quantity of the specified fruit on their worksheet. The teacher repeats instructions clearly, emphasizing numbers and fruit names.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Hold Up (Semi-controlled Drill):</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of fruits. The teacher says, "I want [number] [fruit name], please." (e.g., "I want five pineapples, please." Teacher explicitly models the /p/ sound in pineapple). Students listen and hold up the corresponding number of fingers and point to the correct fruit flashcard the teacher is showing. This activity reinforces phonemic awareness of numbers and fruit names.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Check (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of fruit flashcards and number cards. Student A (the 'shopper') closes their eyes. Student B (the 'speaker') chooses one fruit flashcard and a number card. Student B then says, "I want [number] [fruit], please." Student A listens and opens their eyes, then points to the chosen fruit flashcard and number card. Student B confirms if A listened correctly. They swap roles after 2-3 turns. The teacher monitors for correct listening and identification.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Market Basket Listening Game" (a guided simulation). Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group has a 'market stall' (a designated area on their desk) with various fruit cutouts (apples, bananas, oranges, pineapples – at least 5-10 of each) and a small 'basket'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher acts as the 'customer' for the entire class, or designates one student to be the 'customer' for their group.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher (or 'customer') says a shopping request, e.g., "Hello, I want four bananas and two oranges, please." (emphasizing the /b/ sound in 'bananas').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students in each group listen carefully. One designated student from the group (the 'shopper') quickly picks the correct number of fruit cutouts from their 'market stall' and places them into their group's 'basket'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then asks, "How many bananas did you buy?" and students verify their basket. The teacher also checks for polite language.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity continues with different requests using all target vocabulary and numbers.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> This activity focuses on fluent listening comprehension and following multi-step instructions within the shopping scenario. Students collaborate by ensuring the correct items are picked, reinforcing understanding of quantity and fruit names.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "My Shopping List Check." Each student receives a blank 'shopping basket' drawn on a paper. The teacher reads out a short "shopping list" of three items with quantities (e.g., "I want three apples, one banana, and two pineapples, please."). Students listen and draw the correct number of each fruit inside their shopping basket. The teacher repeats the list twice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Correctly identifies the number of items (e.g., draws 3 apples).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Correctly identifies the specific fruit (e.g., draws apples, not oranges).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary (Receptive):</b> Demonstrates understanding of 'money', 'buy', 'store', 'basket' through actions or drawings.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation Awareness:</b> Reacts appropriately to the /b/ and /p/ sounds, distinguishing words like "banana" and "pineapple".</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can understand (circle one): 1 fruit / 2 fruits / 3 fruits when I listen."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the (draw one fruit from today's lesson)."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> The teacher prompts students with two guiding questions:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about shopping did you learn today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when listening to the shopping lists?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening-focused lesson? (e.g., TPR, visual aids, clarity of instructions, student engagement with the shopping game).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support listening comprehension for all students? (e.g., more repetitions, slower speech, pairing stronger with weaker listeners).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully identify the main idea from short spoken passages and participate in call-and-response activities?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we listened to shop for fruits. Next time, we will read a short story about going to the market to buy these same delicious fruits!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>